#!/usr/bin/python3
"""Route MultiMedia1 to both speakers once the ROG5 sound card is up.

Run by rog5-audio.service after the audio modules load and by
rog5-audio-route.service after ALSA restored its saved state. The card has no
UCM profile, so nothing else sets it up: without this, playback reaches no
amplifier.
  MultiMedia1 (card 0, device 0) -> SENARY MI2S -> CS35L45 x2
  left (ASP_RX1) -> the top amplifier (RCV), right (ASP_RX2) -> the bottom (SPK),
  as on stock: in landscape with the side port down the top edge is on the left
Both amplifiers run in speaker mode at 19 dBV analog (3, the highest step, as
stock and the driver default). The digital level depends on the path: 0 dB
(409; 0.25 dB steps) only on an amplifier whose protection DSP was started and
verified (below), else -12 dB (361, the level of the kernels before the
protection work, e.g. the r69 fallback). Per amplifier the route first sets
-12 dB and confirms it (attenuate(): two value-changing writes that both
succeed, so both reach the amplifier, then the read-back), and only then
touches its DACPCM Source, its DSP or its AMP Enable Switch; it raises an
amplifier to 0 dB only after DACPCM Source = DSP_TX1 is confirmed (the DAPM
mux put succeeds even when its register write fails, and repeating a value
the register cache already holds writes nothing, so set_mux() goes through
another item first and checks the kernel log). If -12 dB cannot be confirmed, that amplifier fails
closed: AMP Enable Switch off and DACPCM Source = Zero, its DSP left alone,
and the run fails (a later run retries). If it cannot be muted either,
MultiMedia1 is disconnected from SENARY MI2S and nothing else is set. The
playback mixer is switched on last. DAPM powers the amplifiers only while a
stream runs.

Speaker protection: when install-rog5-speaker-firmware has installed the
vendor firmware and this unit's calibration, and /etc/rog5/speaker-dsp.conf
enables the amplifier (amps=rcv spk; missing or empty: none; the installer
writes amps= so nothing changes until the bring-up enables it) and the
calibration came from persist (allow_default_calibration=1 accepts the vendor
default 8392), each enabled amplifier plays through
its CS35L45 DSP as on stock: DSP_RX1/2 = its ASP channel, DSP_RX5/6/7 =
VDD_BATTMON/VDD_BSTMON/CLASSH_TGT (stock driver workaround for this
firmware), firmware preloaded, calibration (CAL_R/CAL_STATUS/CAL_CHECKSUM,
written by the kernel from the installed .bin before the core starts) read
back and checked, CSPL_STATE/CSPL_ERRORNO checked, optionally the stock
"music" tuning delta (music_delta=1), and only then DACPCM Source = DSP_TX1.
The amplifier then stays runtime-active, so its DSP pauses between streams as
on stock instead of hibernating (hibernate=1 allows the driver's hibernation).
Any failure puts that amplifier back on the direct path (DACPCM = ASP_RX, DSP
stopped).

A running DSP is normally kept, but not one the kernel log shows wedged: a
pause or resume its firmware did not acknowledge ("Failed to set mailbox cmd",
"PRE_PMD: <amp> DSP1 event failed", "Protection firmware did not pause")
after the DSP's last start ("Protection firmware calibration CAL_R"), nor one
whose last start is no longer in the log (unreadable, or overwritten). Such a
firmware stops answering its mailbox and plays silence, while the controls
still read CSPL_STATE 0 (2026-10-01: a PCM opened and closed within ~100 ms
during boot; kernels with 0151 reload it themselves). That DSP is power
cycled (Preload off, then the normal start) once playback is idle: the
preload switch cannot stop a DSP that a running stream holds up. If playback
does not stop within PCM_IDLE_SECONDS, the amplifier goes to the direct path.
Without the firmware, with the amplifier disabled, on a kernel without the
protection support (no DSP controls) or after a failed start, it plays ASP_RX
straight to the DAC (no protection) at -12 dB.

With the mic DTB feature, the built-in microphones record on PCM 3
(MultiMedia3 <- VA_CODEC_DMA_TX_0): DMIC0 (stock "dmic1", handset) left,
DMIC2 (stock "dmic2", speaker side) right, +18 dB decimator gain as on stock.
Those controls are optional: without the VA macro they are skipped.

"audio-route --sanitize-alsa-state" (89-rog5-alsa-state.rules, before
90-alsa-restore.rules) removes this card's DSP, DSP_RXn and DACPCM entries
from /var/lib/alsa/asound.state, so "alsactl restore" can neither start a DSP
nor write stale coefficients into one; this script owns those controls. It
also rewrites the saved Digital PCM Volume to -12 dB and the AMP Enable
Switches to off: a shutdown behind a running protection DSP saves 0 dB, the
register default of DACPCM Source is the unprotected ASP_RX1, and the route
service runs only after the restore. A state file it cannot parse (only the
layout alsactl store writes is understood) or rewrite is moved aside
(asound.state.rog5-unsafe), so nothing unchecked is restored.
Uses libasound through ctypes, as amixer cset would.
"""
import fcntl
import glob
import os
import re
import sys
import time

